While I cannot speak about Whaite Group Press, I do have a few words to 
say about the others.

While I found 'C++ for Dummies' a perfectly readable and fairly thorough 
covering of C++, enjoyable to read and covering the more complex issues 
of C++ thoroughly without skimping, 'C for Dummies' was a completely 
different matter.

I went through it with a pencil and a not-very-fine-toothed comb, 
correcting the mistakes. There were DOZENS. I immediately went out and 
bought myself a proper manual instead (Try 'The C programming Language' 
by Kernighan and Ritchie, if you want a proper reference manual, or the 
[your flavour of unix clone] man pages).  Basically, the only reason I 
wanted to correct C for Dummies was a friend wanted to borrow it, and I 
preffered that they passed their exams without stressing over incorrect 
information. I no longer have this book.

I may be maligning them: they may have brought out a new version with the 
errors corrected, or the US version may differ from the UK one.

And as for Sams.Net... well, let's just say they have a SERIOUS problem 
with typos (in the code examples) in the books I have read. And their 
indexing is appalling, as is the book layout. (Hrm... the only name I can 
remember is "Learn CGI programming in Perl in 7 days", which I do still 
have, but only because I can't yet get the O'reilly CGI book). I do not 
intend to buy any more from this company.

Books I would reccomend? Microsoft Press generally write good books on
programming style (shame the MS programmers never read them!). I would say
that their two flagship books ("Writing Solid Code", and even more, "Code
Complete") should be in every programmer's library, and should be read at
least once. they might help to prevent the bug-levels that are currently 
the norm.

O'Reilly have got to be the best by a long shot, being both the most
readable, without being couched in idiotspeak, packed with valid examples
and useful points, and with good indexes to boot. (I have "Learning Perl" 
[a good Perl basics book, and a nice accompaniment to the camel book) and
"Java in a Nutshell" [The best of all the Java books I have read], and one
other I forget the name of. I also have the camel book on order [their
main Perl book, compulsory for all PERL programmers], and intend to get
their CGI book as soon as I get the money. 

Also take a look at the Que reference books, which are damn fine reference
manuals in general, with very good indexes. Not exactly hot on
readability, but the best for thoroughness I have found if you want to
find out something in a hurry. Their "Dos Programmers Reference" is
compulsory for all people programming at anything other than the highest
level in DOS. 

I'm sorry I haven't been able to give you more book titles, but there you
go, I'm crap with names. (I love our local Dillon's, which has a
knowledgeable and well-read in-house computer guy for the computer
department, so when I walk in and ask for the 'camel book', he knows
exactly what I mean!)

